<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Surendra Sai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1884">1884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Surendra Sai was a claimant to the throne of Sambalpur in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, who led a long armed revolt against British rule. Imprisoned at Hazaribagh from 1840, he was freed by mutineers in 1857 and fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war in the hills of Sambalpur until his surrender in 1862. Rearrested in 1864, he died in Asirgarh fort in 1884 and is honoured in Odisha as a freedom fighter.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Surendra Sai was born on 23 January 1809 in the village of Khinda, about 40 km north of Sambalpur, one of the seven children of Dharma Singh. He belonged to the ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan</a> clan of Sambalpur State, through the branch descended from Prince Aniruddha Sai, brother of an earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a>. Only a man could rule Sambalpur, and the childless Maharaj Sai is said to have chosen Surendra Sai as his successor. The British refused the claim, since he sought full independent rule.</p><p>When Maharaj Sai died in 1827 the British installed his widow, Rani Mohan Kumari, against the custom that only men ruled. Her land revenue policy alienated the Gond and Binjhal tribal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a>, and the British replaced her with Narayan Singh, a member of the royal family born of a low <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, again passing over Surendra Sai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Surendra Sai began resisting the British at the age of 18 in 1827, with the Gond zamindars as his closest allies. In 1840 he was captured with his brother Udyanta Sai and his uncle Balaram Singh, who died in Hazaribagh Jail. When Narayan Singh died without a male heir in 1849, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a> annexed Sambalpur under the Doctrine of Lapse. During the rising of 1857 sepoys freed Surendra Sai after 17 years in prison, and he carried the struggle into the hills, supported by brothers, sons, relatives and zamindars.</p><p>The rebellion elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> had collapsed by the end of 1858, but he fought on. Major Forster, given full military and civil powers, was removed in 1861 after three years without success. His successor, Major Impey, seized the rebels' food stocks and then turned to a policy of conciliation, and Surendra Sai surrendered soon after Sambalpur passed to the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces</a> on 30 April 1862.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Impey's death the administration turned hostile again and arrested Surendra Sai with his relations and followers; he and six companions were held in the Asirgarh hill fort, where he spent 19 years and died in 1884. Many of his associates were executed, and Hatte Singh died in the Cellular Jail in the Andamans. Sambalpur is counted among the last districts outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> that the British occupied. A statue of him was placed at the Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 2005, and a university of technology at Burla, a medical college and the Jharsuguda airport bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Veer Surendra Sai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A claimant, <b>Surendra Sai</b>, led an armed rebellion from 1857 until his surrender in 1862.</blockquote><a href="/state/sambalpur-state">Sambalpur State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sambalpur district, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Asurgarh, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.47,83.98&amp;year=1857" aria-label="Sambalpur district on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6805 1370 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Surendra Sai"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7255 1640h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7255 1640h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.47,83.98&amp;year=1857">What was here in 1857? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
